olf and skiing in Corpo1 is a clue. It's about capturing the \"leisure class\" of the post-pandemic era.\n\n       Drafting - Segment 1 (The Logic):* Start with the logic of the pivot. It's not just about selling a jacket; it's about selling a membership to a specific social stratum. The diversification (golf, ski) isn't a side project; it's an ecosystem.\n       Drafting - Segment 2 (The Materiality):* Discuss the choice of materials (Linen, Sea Island cotton) not as a technical detail, but as a cultural signifier. In a world of fast Fashion and digital noise, the \"tactile\" becomes the new luxury.\n       Drafting - Segment 3 (The Economic Angle):* The jump to 60 million. It’s a calculated expansion. The physical retail presence (after Rome) is the anchor.\n\n       Check against constraints:*\n           No repetition of Corpo1: Corpo1 mentioned the materials and the golf/ski lines. I need to frame them differently—not as what they are, but why* they are being used as a strategy.\n           Word count:* Aiming for ~375 words.\n           Tone check: Is it too \"marketing\"? Yes, I need to make it more \"Riva.\" It needs to analyze the reason* behind the strategy.\n           Closing:* \"Se la strategia di diversificazione verso i segmenti del tempo libero premium continuerà a sostenere i margini con questa progressione, il passaggio da brand tecnico a player dello lifestyle globale diventerà un modello imitatore per il segmento luxury medio-alto.\" (Wait, the prompt says: \"se quel fenomeno culturale continua a crescere con questi numeri, tra due anni il dibattito mainstream non potrà più ignorarlo, nel bene o nel male\" — I should adapt this structure).\n\n       Opening:* The expansion from technical outerwear to \"lifestyle\" is a move to capture the \"leisure economy.\"\n       Middle:* The use of Sea Island cotton and linen isn't just about quality; it's about the \"slow\" aesthetic in a fast world. It's the \"Battiato-esque\" precision applied to a garmen